     1	# R1 methods governance (#1372)
     2	
     3	**Recorded:** 2026-10-01. **Issue:** #1372, parent epic #1370.
     4	**Authority:** the maintainer decisions of 2026-10-01 recorded in
     5	`r0-authorization.md` and `amendment-001-public-proxy.md` (R0, #1371). This
     6	file defers to those records for caps, deadlines, and permitted access. If
     7	they are absent, unmerged, or inconsistent with this file, R1 is not `MET`.
     8	
     9	**Rules governed:** [r1-decision-rules-v1.md](r1-decision-rules-v1.md).
    10	
    11	## 1. What replaces the independent human reviewer
    12	
    13	#1372 originally requires a consenting independent **human** methods reviewer.
    14	Only the maintainer and AI agents are available. On 2026-10-01 the maintainer
    15	decided that R1 methods governance is performed by cross-family AI adversarial
    16	review instead. Consequences, stated plainly:
    17	
    18	- R1 cannot reach the `MET` value as #1372 was originally written. Any `MET`
    19	  recorded here is **"MET (AI-adjudicated)"** under the maintainer's
    20	  substitution, and every downstream output carries the label
    21	  *AI-adjudicated, public-proxy domain*.
    22	- This is **not independent human review**. It does not satisfy any
    23	  requirement elsewhere (#1254, #1259, #1284-#1309, Draft v3 section 7.1) that
    24	  names an independent human reviewer, adopter lead, or non-maintainer
    25	  participant.
    26	
    27	## 2. Roles
    28	
    29	| Role | Holder | Function |
    30	|---|---|---|
    31	| Proposer | Claude (Anthropic), via Claude Code | Drafts rules; dispositions objections |
    32	| Adversarial methods reviewer | OpenAI Codex CLI (`codex exec`), read-only sandbox | Hostile methods review; countersigns by stating "no blocking objections" against a specific artifact SHA |
    33	| Third check | GitHub Copilot pull-request review | Advisory; its comments are dispositioned in the PR but it cannot countersign |
    34	| Decision and merge authority | Maintainer @juanmicrosoft | Decides rejected blocking objections (section 6); merges |
    35	
    36	## 3. Reviewer identity (configuration, not a person)
    37	
    38	The "identity" of the reviewer is its configuration. Each round file records
    39	the actual values; the template values are:
    40	
    41	| Field | Value |
    42	|---|---|
    43	| Tool | OpenAI Codex CLI, `codex exec` |
    44	| Tool version | Recorded per round (`codex --version`; 0.159.2 at drafting) |
    45	| Model | Recorded per round from the CLI session header; the CLI default under `--ignore-user-config` (`gpt-6.1-sol` at drafting), unchanged across rounds where possible |
    46	| Reasoning effort | `high` (`-c model_reasoning_effort="high"`) for review rounds |
    47	| Sandbox | `--sandbox read-only` |
    48	| Other flags | `--ephemeral` (no session persistence), `--ignore-user-config` (no user plugins or notifier); Codex `memories` feature disabled, so no cross-session memory |
    49	| Working directory | This repository's worktree checked out at the round's artifact SHA |
    50	| Prompt | [reviews/r1/codex-prompt-template.md](reviews/r1/codex-prompt-template.md); its `sha256` is recorded in each round file. Round-specific additions (prior dispositions) are appended verbatim and recorded |
    51	| Network | Not relied on; the reviewer is told to read local files only |
    52	
    53	A change of model or tool version between rounds is recorded and does not by
    54	itself invalidate earlier rounds; the countersigning round is the one whose
    55	configuration is cited.
    56	
    57	## 4. Competence, independence, and conflicts
    58	
    59	**Competence.** Neither AI reviewer has verified credentials in clinical-trial
    60	or econometric methods. Their competence is unmeasured beyond a single
    61	planted-defect probe (section 5.3). The rules compensate by making R4's
    62	estimator falsifiable (type I, Monte Carlo, and reproducibility checks in
    63	rules section 4.2) rather than trusting reviewer judgment about it.
    64	
    65	**Independence.**
    66	
    67	| Risk | Assessment | Mitigation |
    68	|---|---|---|
    69	| Shared training data | Claude and Codex models are trained on overlapping public text, including the same statistics literature; they may share blind spots and stock answers | Cross-vendor review reduces, but does not remove, correlated error. Recorded as a limitation |
    70	| Framing by proposer | Claude writes the review prompt and the dispositions the reviewer sees | Fixed, hashed prompt template; reviewer reads the artifacts and the original v0.20 records directly instead of a Claude summary; all dispositions are published verbatim |
    71	| Same-vendor overlap in the third check | Copilot may run OpenAI models, so it is not independent of Codex | Copilot is advisory only |
    72	| Sycophancy / leniency | An AI reviewer may stop objecting under repeated rounds or authoritative-sounding rebuttals | Rejected blocking objections cannot be closed by Claude alone (section 6); the round cap is 5; the competence probe tests whether the reviewer flags planted defects |
    73	| Self-review | Claude cannot review its own rules as an independent party | Claude is proposer only and never countersigns |
    74	
    75	**Conflicts of interest.**
    76	
    77	- The maintainer created Calor and benefits from a favorable result. The
    78	  maintainer also authored the substitution of AI review for human review and
    79	  controls merge. This is a material conflict. It is disclosed, and the rules
    80	  limit it: the maintainer may not overrule a blocking objection into a
    81	  countersignature (section 6), and the thresholds cannot be amended
    82	  (rules section 10).
    83	- Claude (Anthropic) and Codex (OpenAI) have no financial stake in Calor known
    84	  to this record. Their vendors' commercial interest in AI-assisted coding is a
    85	  diffuse conflict and is disclosed.
    86	
    87	## 5. Adversarial review protocol
    88	
    89	### 5.1 Rounds
    90	
    91	1. Record the artifact commit SHA and the `sha256` of each reviewed file.
    92	2. Run Codex with the template prompt plus, from round 2, the prior round's
    93	   objections and their dispositions.
    94	3. Codex emits numbered objections, each with severity `blocking`, `major`,
    95	   or `minor`, and ends with either `VERDICT: no blocking objections` or
    96	   `VERDICT: blocking objections remain`.
    97	4. Claude dispositions every objection: **accepted and fixed** (with the
    98	   change) or **rejected** (with an explicit reason).
    99	5. Stop when a round has zero blocking objections, or after round 5.
   100	6. Each Codex call is appended to [spend-ledger.md](spend-ledger.md).
   101	
   102	Round files: `reviews/r1/round-<N>-codex.md` (raw Codex output plus the
   103	disposition table).
   104	
   105	### 5.2 Scope of review
   106	
   107	The reviewer looks for rules that bias toward Calor, undefined or post-hoc
   108	adjustable thresholds, missing error control, missingness laundering
   109	(treating missing or invalid as success or as zero demand), ambiguous
   110	classifications, untestable criteria, and divergence from the original v0.20
   111	claim. The reviewer must not query public repository task data
   112	(rules section 9).
   113	
   114	### 5.3 Competence probe
   115	
   116	Before round 1, Codex reviews a short planted-defect rule set
   117	([reviews/r1/competence-probe.md](reviews/r1/competence-probe.md)) containing
   118	known defects. The result is recorded in that file. A reviewer that misses
   119	most planted defects is still used (no alternative exists), but the
   120	limitation is recorded and weakens the countersignature's evidentiary value.
   121	
   122	### 5.4 Budget
   123	
   124	At most 5 rounds per artifact, within the overall approximately USD 200 API
   125	cap and the 2026-10-29 window set by R0. Exceeding either ends R1 as
   126	`EXPIRED`.
   127	
   128	## 6. Recusal and rejected objections
   129	
   130	- Claude **cannot be final arbiter** of its own rejection of a **blocking**
   131	  objection. A rejected blocking objection is re-presented to Codex in the
   132	  next round with the rejection reason. If Codex withdraws or downgrades it,
   133	  the rejection stands.
   134	- If Codex maintains a blocking objection after rejection, or round 5 ends
   135	  with it unresolved, it goes to the maintainer, whose explicit decision is
   136	  recorded in `reviews/r1/countersignature.md`:
   137	  - **accept**: the objection must be fixed, which requires another round
   138	    (if rounds remain); or
   139	  - **overrule**: recorded with reasons; R1 is then **not `MET`**, because the
   140	    countersignature requires zero blocking objections. The maintainer cannot
   141	    convert an overrule into a countersignature.
   142	- Rejected `major` and `minor` objections are dispositioned by Claude with
   143	  reasons and published; they do not block.
   144	
   145	## 7. Countersignature format
   146	
   147	R1 is `MET (AI-adjudicated)` only when all of the following are recorded:
   148	
   149	1. A Codex round output containing `VERDICT: no blocking objections`, against
   150	   artifact commit `<SHA>`, with the `sha256` of the normative set:
   151	   `r1-decision-rules-v1.md`, `r1-decision-rules-v1.json`, this file, and
   152	   `reviews/r1/codex-prompt-template.md`.
   153	2. The maintainer's merge of a PR whose merged content for those files has the
   154	   same `sha256` values.
   155	3. No unresolved maintainer overrule under section 6.
   156	
   157	Terminal values otherwise: `UNAVAILABLE` (5 rounds without zero blocking
   158	objections, or Codex unavailable), `EXPIRED` (window or cap exhausted),
   159	`REVOKED` / `INVALIDATED` per the #1370 lifecycle amendments, or
   160	`NOT_REACHED` if R0 is not `MET`.
   161	
